WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.083
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:16.436
Unit twelve: Media Studies. Today you will explain causes and consequences in the media with formal connectors.

4
00:00:16.736 --> 00:00:19.214
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.980
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:25.922
Mass media.

7
00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:32.434
Mass media means channels such as TV, newspapers and the internet that reach large audiences.

8
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.131
Mass media shape public opinion.

9
00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:38.624
Your turn. Say: Mass media.

10
00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:42.665
Mass media.

11
00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:44.812
Representation.

12
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:49.326
Representation means the way a group or issue is portrayed.

13
00:00:49.917 --> 00:00:53.669
The representation of rural people is often stereotyped.

14
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:57.040
Your turn. Say: Representation.

15
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.191
Representation.

16
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.951
Audience.

17
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.948
Audience means the people a media text is aimed at.

18
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.130
Each channel targets a different audience.

19
00:01:10.717 --> 00:01:13.276
Your turn. Say: Audience.

20
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.158
Audience.

21
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:19.045
Gatekeeping.

22
00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:24.186
Gatekeeping means the process of deciding which information reaches the public.

23
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:27.015
Editors perform a gatekeeping role.

24
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:30.362
Your turn. Say: Gatekeeping.

25
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.306
Gatekeeping.

26
00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.225
Agenda-setting.

27
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:41.469
Agenda-setting means the media's power to decide which issues seem important.

28
00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:45.221
Agenda-setting explains why some crises are ignored.

29
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:48.272
Your turn. Say: Agenda-setting.

30
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.285
Agenda-setting.

31
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.086
Consumption.

32
00:01:55.083 --> 00:01:58.070
Consumption means the act of using or watching media.

33
00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:01.345
News consumption has moved to mobile phones.

34
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.637
Your turn. Say: Consumption.

35
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.652
Consumption.

36
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:10.360
Bias.

37
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.302
Bias means an unfair preference for one side.

38
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:17.648
The report showed a clear bias toward the government.

39
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.554
Your turn. Say: Bias.

40
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:24.285
Bias.

41
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:26.121
Literacy.

42
00:02:27.117 --> 00:02:30.823
Literacy means the ability to understand and evaluate texts.

43
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.648
Media literacy should be taught at school.

44
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.937
Your turn. Say: Literacy.

45
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:40.859
Literacy.

46
00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:45.468
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.317 --> 00:02:50.197
Visal, why did the floods in the north receive so little coverage last month?

48
00:02:51.283 --> 00:02:58.916
Because of agenda-setting. The election dominated the news, so the floods were pushed off the front pages.

49
00:03:00.017 --> 00:03:01.645
Is that a form of bias?

50
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:14.412
Not deliberate bias, perhaps, but gatekeeping has consequences. Owing to limited airtime, editors must choose, and rural stories often lose.

51
00:03:15.517 --> 00:03:17.574
What can ordinary viewers do about it?

52
00:03:18.683 --> 00:03:28.058
Develop media literacy. As a result of comparing several sources, we notice not only how stories are told but which ones are missing.

53
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.596
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:34.785
Grammar focus: Cause and effect connectors.

55
00:03:35.085 --> 00:03:36.283
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:40.272
Because of agenda-setting, certain stories receive more coverage.

57
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:44.599
Owing to limited airtime, editors must choose carefully.

58
00:03:45.483 --> 00:03:48.574
As a result of comparing sources, we notice what is missing.

59
00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:59.105
'Because of / owing to / due to / as a result of' are followed by a noun or -ing, never a full clause. 'Because' + clause; 'consequently / therefore' begin a new sentence with the effect.

60
00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.209
Be careful! Don't say: Because agenda-setting, some stories get coverage.

61
00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:09.908
Say: Because of agenda-setting, some stories get coverage.

62
00:04:10.208 --> 00:04:11.882
Use 'because of' + noun.

63
00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:15.249
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:17.754
Who Decides What Is News?.

65
00:04:18.054 --> 00:04:22.225
The news we consume each day is the result of countless editorial decisions.

66
00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:28.890
Because of limited space and time, gatekeepers select a small fraction of possible stories.

67
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:36.462
Agenda-setting research suggests that the media may not tell us what to think, but it strongly influences what we think about.

68
00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:42.740
Owing to the rise of social media, algorithms now perform much of this gatekeeping invisibly.

69
00:04:43.733 --> 00:04:48.368
Consequently, media literacy has become a civic skill rather than an academic one.

70
00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:53.515
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.185
blank limited airtime, the story was cut.

72
00:04:57.485 --> 00:05:01.325
A, Owing to. B, Because. C, Therefore.

73
00:05:04.867 --> 00:05:06.924
The answer is A: Owing to.

74
00:05:08.083 --> 00:05:09.769
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:17.055
A, Because of the rain was heavy, we stayed.. B, Because the rain was heavy, we stayed.. C, Because of heavy, we stayed..

76
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:24.120
The answer is B: Because the rain was heavy, we stayed..

77
00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:29.036
The media's power to decide which issues seem important is blank.

78
00:05:29.336 --> 00:05:34.116
A, gatekeeping. B, consumption. C, agenda-setting.

79
00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:40.049
The answer is C: agenda-setting.

80
00:05:41.183 --> 00:05:43.032
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:43.332 --> 00:05:47.810
A, show a bias. B, do a bias. C, make bias of.

82
00:05:51.367 --> 00:05:54.074
The answer is A: show a bias.

83
00:05:55.217 --> 00:05:58.505
According to the reading, who now performs much gatekeeping?

84
00:05:58.805 --> 00:06:02.841
A, readers. B, algorithms. C, governments.

85
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.341
The answer is B: algorithms.

86
00:06:09.533 --> 00:06:12.627
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.483 --> 00:06:17.399
What is one way media literacy could help you evaluate the news?

88
00:06:34.183 --> 00:06:35.672
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.972 --> 00:07:09.027
One way media literacy helps me is by encouraging me to check who produced a story and why. Because of this habit, I compare at least two sources before sharing anything important. For example, a viral post about a dangerous medicine turned out to come from a site selling an alternative product. Some people say this takes too much time. However, as a result of a few minutes of checking, we can avoid spreading misinformation to hundreds of people.

90
00:07:09.327 --> 00:07:11.512
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:12.417 --> 00:07:17.112
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and cause and effect connectors.

92
00:07:17.412 --> 00:07:22.654
Today's value is media literacy. Ask who made the message, and why, before you believe it.

93
00:07:22.954 --> 00:07:25.885
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:26.185 --> 00:07:28.733
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

